{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B027",
    "B012"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B027 to B046. Quantum error correction remains a foundational achievement, but the discussion has sharpened the distinction between a framework whose decisive scalable realization remains incomplete and an experimental method that has already opened a new, controllable precision-measurement regime. Magic-wavelength confinement permits interrogation of many atoms while preserving the clock transition, and optical lattice clocks have demonstrated extraordinary performance reproducibly across laboratories. Katori and Ye provide a compact, complementary attribution linking the decisive concept to defining high-performance realization. The case rests on the physical method itself, not on a prospective redefinition of the second or on downstream applications.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary must be kept strict: optical lattice clocks are not synonymous with all optical-frequency-clock development, and the respective indispensable contributions of the broader metrology community require archival confirmation. B027 is more radical in its implication for the physical possibility of reliable large quantum systems; my change reflects timing and attribution, not a lesser judgment of its conceptual depth.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that magic-wavelength confinement, Katori's concept, Ye's defining experimental advances, and independent reproducibility make lattice clocks an unusually mature and clearly attributable achievement.",
        "response": "I agree, especially with separating the demonstrated physical advance from any future standards decision. From the perspective of quantum control, the key fact is that the method robustly removes a central measurement limitation while retaining the useful atomic coherence. That is a sufficiently distinct and repeatedly realized new regime to merit priority now."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth argues that B027 should remain a strong future candidate because a fully fault-tolerant logical computer has not yet been realized, whereas lattice clocks have already established their central method at extraordinary accuracy.",
        "response": "I accept this timing judgment. I continue to regard error correction as physics rather than merely an engineering promise, but the remaining gap is precisely whether logical protection can be maintained and scaled under the full demands of fault tolerance. B046 does not require that extrapolation: its defining quantum-control principle is already experimentally secure."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson favors WMAP because its observed consequences are settled, and he asks that incomplete realization remain material when assessing quantum error correction.",
        "response": "His maturity criterion is persuasive even though I retain a different preferred field. B046 combines the empirical security he seeks with a much narrower two-person attribution than a mission-scale achievement. The citation should consequently remain limited to the lattice-clock invention and realization, rather than claim the whole history of precision cosmology or metrology."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone are the appropriate restricted attribution for the invention and defining realization of optical lattice clocks.",
      "How the citation can distinguish lattice clocks cleanly from the wider optical-clock field without understating indispensable prior atomic-clock and laser advances.",
      "Whether future logical-qubit results will soon make B027 the more timely recognition of the foundational error-correction framework."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. As director of WACQT, I retain a general professional connection to quantum computing, including the alternative B027, but no direct connection to its named laureates is apparent."
  }
}
